          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em symmetric monoidal category
            is a category with a product operation – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            – for which the product is
            = succeed "." do
              %em as commutative as possible
          %p
            The point is that there are different degrees to which higher categorical products may be commutative. While a bare
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            is either commutative or not, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            may be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
            – which already means that the order of products may be reversed up to some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            – without being
            %em symmetric
            monoidal – which means that changing the order of a product twice, from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \otimes b
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \otimes a
            back to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \otimes b
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
            to the original.
          %p
            For higher monoidal categories there are accordingly ever more shades of the notion of “commutativity” of the monoidal product. This is described in detail at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+monoidal+n-category"} k-tuply monoidal n-category
          %p
            In general, the term
            %em symmetric monoidal
            is used for the maximally commutative case. See for instance
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} symmetric monoidal (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
            is, under the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} connective spectrum
          %p
            A symmetric monoidal category is a special case of the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+pseudomonoid"} symmetric pseudomonoid
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sylleptic+monoidal+2-category"} sylleptic monoidal 2-category
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong symmetric monoidal category
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
              for which the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi y
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B_{x,y} \colon x \otimes y \to y \otimes x
            %p satisfies the condition:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    B_{y,x} \circ B_{x,y} = 1_{x \otimes y}
            %p
              for all objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
          %p
            Intuitively this says that switching things twice
            %em in the same direction
            has no effect.
          %p
            Expanding this out a bit: a
            %strong symmetric monoidal category
            is, to begin with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            equipped with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo ×
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes : M \times M \to M
          %p
            called the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong tensor product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \in M
          %p
            called the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong unit object
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %mi z
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi z
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_{x,y,z} : (x \otimes y) \otimes z \to x \otimes (y \otimes z)
          %p
            called the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong associator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi λ
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_x : 1 \otimes x \to x
          %p
            called the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong left unitor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ρ
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_x : x \otimes 1 \to x
          %p
            called the
            = succeed "," do
              %strong right unitor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_{x,y} : x \otimes y \to y \otimes x
          %p
            called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong braiding
            = succeed "," do
              %strong pentagon identity
