          %h3#grothendieck_ring Grothendieck ring
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            naturally has the structure of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian
            monoidal category that of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braided monoidal category
            that of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            and, finally, of an abelian symmetric monoidal category that of a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lambda-ring"} Lambda-ring
          %h3#internal_logic Internal logic
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"}> closed
            ) symmetric monoidal categories is called
            = succeed "." do
